olled drug  abuse deaths  which using by using all of those tools  we may have available  in that arrow quiver analogy i gave a  little while ago  so furthering then in terms of public  health  and what a responsible program's  state medical program would include for  responsible legislation  i like to think of this in terms of what  things could be in responsible  legislation that  impact a given patient's outcome for the  better  and for those of you who may or may not  be able to read on your phone or laptop  these seven points i'm just going to  read them one at a time  by telling you that there are states now  that are considering  these seven potential things to add to  their state's legislation  that ultimately would be regarded as a  federal necessity once cannabis comes  out of federal prohibition  and the first is developing a  responsible  initial standard of care from which  physicians entering the space  can practice medicine by so  practitioner oversight by boards of  medicine  have to have an initial standard of care  rather than having a standard of care  developed from  a lowest common accepted standard  it needs to be regarded as having what  would be the next higher standard  that physicians can provide in this  understanding  that it's an evolving field second would  be  continuous quality improvement now what  do i mean by that  continuous quality improvement metrics  are used in  everything in healthcare currently  hospitals  nursing homes pharmacies everything in  health care goes through an  accreditation  process whereby when there is an  unfavorable  outcome that information is shared  proactively throughout healthcare so  that we  try to make it so that we redesign  things so as not to repeat  that unfavorable patient outcome  and to a certain extent legislators that  ignore the importance of an  accreditation program that approaches  health care this way through continuous  quality improvement  could be hard-pressed to explain why  without this they were allowing bad  outcomes to recur  we don't hear much about too many bad  outcomes yet but as data becomes tracked  i'm certain we'll be hearing about more  the third item here is to initially  constrain conditions  by lending the evidence-based data of  these conclusive high-quality  findings that are conclusive or  substantial  for what it actually works for and we  covered those before  now that doesn't dismiss anecdotal  reports which become the basis  for the hypotheses of future research  but there is a difference with that  evidence-based data versus  anecdotal reports and to recognize  though to initially responsibly and  judiciously  start to release approve things rather  than having  a virtual everything list that fits for  a qualifying condition  i believe is not only a more responsible  way to  to allow this to progress but one in  which the federal government would  embrace rather than allowing disease  states which may not yet have the  benefit of that evidence-based data  then finally on item four  this requires funded research  and in particular with the way cannabis  is understood today  outcomes in children is particularly  important because this  alludes to the importance of what are  called long-term or longitudinal studies  which we have very very few of so it's  not simply for treating a child with  epilepsy right now  but to look at the long-term effects of  when people use cannabis  what are the differences as they mature  and develop  those skills that allow them to deal  with problem  solving that has previously been  connected in  perhaps one of the very few longitudinal  studies that was not perfect  but inferred and concluded that there  could be a drop of intelligence that  would be a great concern  to those children that started at a  young age using cannabis  and continued using it in high doses  consistently for the next  20 years we need these 20 year and more  studies of following one individual to  really determine  the consequences of outcome because we  know very little about  two things concerning modern cannabis  one  is the effective dosing other than  making a general statement  that the current evidence-based data  suggests  that we grossly overdose and that most  findings suggest  that minimal doses are most effective  with the possible exception as evidence  builds for ptsd  and the second thing we know very very  little about are the long-term effects  on what's what called down regulating  when we  change the system's normal complement of  receptors by exposing them to cannabis  and cannabinoids on a chronic basis  what we do know that's encouraging is  that this particular receptor system  tends to be very plastic in its nature  which means  that it has a very high ability to  recover its full complement of receptors  because a federal study in nida the  national institute of drug abuse  looked at people who were chronic heavy  users  that were sequestered and not given  any cannabis products in a controlled  setting and after only about four weeks  they had a near total return of all  their bodies full complement of these  receptors  that's helpful information to determine  how these receptors may be  affected from long-term use then of  course  if you look at different states the  fifth item here about the full and  accurate  labeling of cannabis products is  absolutely needed to facilitate  meaningful data accession  both for the benefit of researchers as  well as the physician and patient  involved  at that time so you got to know what  you're taking and how often to take it  those particular regulations though  that should have the relatively few  experts  available to modify a given state's  qualifying conditions as the new data  allows is important for keeping up with  that new data  so that you don't have to wait  necessarily for a legislative  body to come to political terms when in  fact health care experts  might be able to add or remove certain  conditions from a given state's  qualifying conditions  and then finally resourcing highly  qualified  guidance in the dispensing and the  thoughtful data processing  of this particular medicine is right now  at a critical state  because the person behind the counter  should  could and in many situations will in the  future  be forced to be a trained individual  that's not to say at this moment  trained pharmacists that are degreed are  are ready to fill this void  but a similar training or oversight  uh should advance in that direction for  expertise  especially that that's what pharmacist  as perhaps  an underutilized and under appreciated  resource in this space  should be better appreciated so finally  then in conclusion  there is a take-home message and the  take-home message is where we are at  politically science-wise legislatively  and that take home message really is  that at this moment in time  cannabis whether used recreationally in  the past present or future  is a temptation for many and it's an  indulgence but it  offers certain choices and those of you  familiar with the genie parable where  you get  two wishes and that genie  gives you that third witch which can be  the gotcha one  we right now are in a position to be  very careful with what we wish for and  how we plan on we'd like to have it  available or regulated  because the message here from the talk  in this keynote is that science and  reason  really do matter so for more information  on this as as uh  you may need i can be reached through  nurturing nature group  that's the uh consultancy that i run  and i'd be happy to hear from you and  hopefully we'll be able to link that way  for future information  thanks for your time
 

